    <briefdescription>
<para>An enumeration of various forms of method body implementations. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para><itemizedlist>
<listitem>
<para><bold>Implicit</bold> - the method body represents a method known to exist for compilation purposes, but is otherwise not present; this is the result of the <javadoccode>into</javadoccode> clause, or the methods of <javadoccode>Object</javadoccode> in the context of an interface, for example; </para>
</listitem>
<listitem>
<para><bold>Declared</bold> - the method body represents a declared but non-implemented method; </para>
</listitem>
<listitem>
<para><bold>Default</bold> - the method body is a default implementation from an interface; </para>
</listitem>
<listitem>
<para><bold>Abstract</bold> - the method body is on a class, but is explicitly abstract; </para>
</listitem>
<listitem>
<para><bold>SansCode</bold> - the method body has no code, but isn&apos;t explicitly abstract; </para>
</listitem>
<listitem>
<para><bold>Capped</bold> - the method body represents the &quot;cap&quot; on a method chain; </para>
</listitem>
<listitem>
<para><bold>Delegating</bold> - the method body is implemented by delegating to the same signature on a different reference; </para>
</listitem>
<listitem>
<para><bold>Field</bold> - the method body represents access to a property&apos;s underlying field, which occurs when a property&apos;s method is overridden and calls <javadoccode>super()</javadoccode>; </para>
</listitem>
<listitem>
<para><bold>Native</bold> - the method body is implemented natively by the runtime; </para>
</listitem>
<listitem>
<para><bold>Explicit</bold> - the method body is represented by byte code that gets executed. </para>
</listitem>
</itemizedlist>
</para>
    </detaileddescription>
